{"id":"lagrange-multipliers-regularization-as-constrain","t":"Norm-constrained least squares and its multiplier","tags":["Optimization","Regression"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"budget_lstsq","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Ridge regression adds a penalty $\\lambda\\lVert w\\rVert^2$. An equivalent view is a hard budget on the weights:\n\n$$\\min_w\\ \\lVert Xw - y\\rVert_2^2 \\quad \\text{subject to} \\quad \\lVert w\\rVert_2 \\le t$$\n\nThe Lagrangian $\\mathcal L(w, \\lambda) = \\lVert Xw - y\\rVert^2 + \\lambda\\,(\\lVert w\\rVert^2 - t^2)$ with $\\lambda \\ge 0$ connects the two. The KKT conditions say the solution is either\n\n- **inactive**: the unconstrained least-squares solution already satisfies $\\lVert w\\rVert \\le t$, and $\\lambda = 0$; or\n- **active**: $\\lambda > 0$ and $w(\\lambda) = (X^\\top X + \\lambda I)^{-1} X^\\top y$, a ridge solution, with $\\lambda$ chosen so that $\\lVert w(\\lambda)\\rVert = t$ exactly.\n\nWrite `budget_lstsq(X, y, t)` that returns `(w, lam)`: the constrained minimiser (float array of shape `(d,)`) and its multiplier (Python `float`).\n\n## Inputs\n- `X`: `(n, d)` with full column rank ($n \\ge d$), `y`: `(n,)`, `t > 0`.\n- Find $\\lambda$ to a relative accuracy of $10^{-10}$ or better.\n- Hint at the design: $\\lVert w(\\lambda)\\rVert$ decreases strictly as $\\lambda$ grows, so a bracketing method is safe. Factor $X$ once (SVD), not once per trial $\\lambda$.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef budget_lstsq(X, y, t):\n    \"\"\"Minimise ||Xw - y||^2 subject to ||w|| <= t. Return (w, Lagrange multiplier).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["First solve without the constraint. If that solution is inside the ball, you are done with $\\lambda = 0$. Otherwise the answer sits on the sphere $\\lVert w\\rVert = t$.","With the SVD $X = USV^\\top$ and $c = S U^\\top y$, the ridge solution is $w(\\lambda) = V\\,\\frac{c}{s^2 + \\lambda}$ and its norm is $\\sqrt{\\sum_i c_i^2/(s_i^2+\\lambda)^2}$: one cheap formula per trial $\\lambda$.","Bisect on $\\lambda \\in [0, \\lVert c\\rVert/t]$; the upper end works because $\\lVert w(\\lambda)\\rVert \\le \\lVert c\\rVert/\\lambda$. Halve the bracket until the midpoint stops changing, then return $w(\\lambda)$ and $\\lambda$."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef budget_lstsq(X, y, t):\n    X, y = np.asarray(X, dtype=float), np.asarray(y, dtype=float)\n    U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(X, full_matrices=False)\n    c = s * (U.T @ y)                                  # X^T y in the right-singular basis\n    w_of = lambda lam: Vt.T @ (c / (s ** 2 + lam))     # ridge solution for any lambda, O(d^2)\n    norm_of = lambda lam: np.linalg.norm(c / (s ** 2 + lam))\n    if norm_of(0.0) <= t:\n        return w_of(0.0), 0.0                          # constraint inactive\n    # ||w(lam)|| <= ||c|| / lam, so lam = ||c|| / t is guaranteed to satisfy the budget\n    lo, hi = 0.0, np.linalg.norm(c) / t\n    for _ in range(200):\n        mid = (lo + hi) / 2\n        if mid in (lo, hi):                            # interval can't shrink further in floating point\n            break\n        lo, hi = (mid, hi) if norm_of(mid) > t else (lo, mid)\n    lam = (lo + hi) / 2\n    return w_of(lam), float(lam)\n","ex_md":"## Why the multiplier is a ridge penalty\nSetting $\\nabla_w \\mathcal L = 2X^\\top(Xw - y) + 2\\lambda w = 0$ gives $(X^\\top X + \\lambda I)w = X^\\top y$: exactly ridge regression with penalty $\\lambda$. Complementary slackness, $\\lambda(\\lVert w\\rVert^2 - t^2) = 0$, says either the budget isn't binding ($\\lambda = 0$) or the solution is on the sphere. So every budget $t$ corresponds to one ridge $\\lambda$, and tighter budgets mean larger $\\lambda$ (compare tests 4, 5 and 6).\n\n## Why bisection is safe\nIn the SVD basis, $\\lVert w(\\lambda)\\rVert^2 = \\sum_i c_i^2/(s_i^2+\\lambda)^2$, a sum of strictly decreasing functions of $\\lambda$. So exactly one $\\lambda > 0$ hits the budget, and any sign change bracket converges. Newton's method on $1/\\lVert w(\\lambda)\\rVert - 1/t$ (the \"secular equation\" used in trust-region solvers) converges much faster, but bisection is simpler to get right and still only needs about 60 cheap $O(d)$ norm evaluations.\n\n## The multiplier as a price\n$\\lambda = -\\frac{1}{2t}\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(\\min \\lVert Xw-y\\rVert^2\\bigr)$: it measures how much the optimal loss would drop per unit of extra budget, scaled. A big $\\lambda$ means the constraint is costing a lot.\n\n## Complexity\nOne SVD, $O(nd^2)$, then $O(d)$ per bisection step and $O(d^2)$ to rebuild $w$.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Solving $(X^\\top X + \\lambda I)w = X^\\top y$ from scratch at every trial $\\lambda$: correct, but $O(d^3)$ each time.\n- Returning a $\\lambda > 0$ when the unconstrained solution already fits (test 1).\n- Using the Lagrangian with $\\frac12$ factors and getting a $\\lambda$ that is off by a factor of 2 from the ridge penalty.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Replace the L2 budget with $\\lVert w\\rVert_1 \\le t$. Why is there no closed form anymore, and what solution path algorithm traces all $t$ at once?\n- Trust-region methods solve exactly this subproblem with a quadratic model of the loss. Where does the budget $t$ come from in that setting?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.]])\narray([3., 4.])\n10.0","out":"(array([3., 4.]), 0.0)"},{"in":"array([[1., 0.],\n       [0., 1.]])\narray([3., 4.])\n1.0","out":"(array([0.6, 0.8]), 3.999999999999999)"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/calculus/critical-points-in-several-variables/","t":"Critical Points in Several Variables","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/applications/linear-regression-with-matrices/","t":"Linear Regression With Matrices","c":"ML Maths"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/how-models-learn/regularisation/","t":"Regularisation","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-maths-course/optimization-for-ml/regularisation-as-optimisation-penalties-constraints-proxima/","t":"Regularisation as optimisation: penalties, constraints, proximal steps","c":"ML Maths"}],"related":["subgradients-for-non-smooth-functions","hessian-matrix-and-convexity-of-linear-regressio","gradient-of-multivariate-linear-regression-loss"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [3.0, 4.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 10.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [3.0, 4.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 0.0]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[1.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [3.0, 4.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.6000000000000001, 0.8000000000000002], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 3.999999999999999]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[2.0, 0.0], [0.0, 1.0]], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2.0, 2.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.0], \"e\": {\"__tup__\": [{\"__nd__\": [0.692820465252779, 0.721110118447192], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1.773501506686268]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [[-0.6664369401110779, 0.09745096576639617, -1.8996468287885815, -1.940069758804618], [-1.4105191377011428, -0.8005887209834909, -0.7341025769963359, -0.45682113475222835], [-0.19085001869622983, -0.8749718100748338, 0.0045788319147004606, 0.5929142852395968], [0.2721900513849475, 0.8811423487101997, 1.8304601977512838, -0.6295367526645378], [1.6024769090509343, -1.0598102529428837, -0.06221362487831411, 1.0090560424996242], [1.3654922615507963, 0.6893418005760346, 0.7105263561444236, 1.3264220939557305], [0.09636664154578385, 0.6949728409923229, -0.6910847613362908, -1.550825416062391], [-2.1468051878572187, 1.5177859664849698, 0.6606450476574494, -0.53164650469696], [-0.6482748071413845, -0.5815600817900254, -1.2175205902753328, -0.8994107523552358], [-0.462558305812066, 1.02522445029034, 0.29648727740128167, -1.5846532084004534], [1.1330275881483045, 0.8493312403985149, 0.14462172530109357, 0.03